<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Hunza State</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1974 CE (approx.) · capital Baltit</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Karakoram principality on the old route to Kashgar, once feared for its caravan raids, whose Mirs ruled from Baltit Fort until 1974.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mirs of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Hunza</a>, of the Ayash dynasty, trace their line to a ruler who, by local tradition, divided the valley with his brother, the ancestor of the Mirs of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagar">Nagar</a>. The register's start date is approximate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltit-fort">Baltit Fort</a>, the royal seat, is several centuries old.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hunza's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burushaski">Burushaski</a>-speaking people farmed irrigated terraces and, until the late 19th century, raided caravans on the routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Ladakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>. The Mirs sent tribute to the Qing emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> while dealing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Most people became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> Muslims. In 1891 a British force defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Hunza</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagar">Nagar</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-safdar-ali-khan">Mir Safdar Ali Khan</a> fled to China, and his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-nazim-khan">Muhammad Nazim Khan</a> was installed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhammad-jamal-khan">Mir Muhammad Jamal Khan</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947. The principality was abolished in 1974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakoram-highway">Karakoram Highway</a> now runs through the valley, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltit-fort">Baltit Fort</a> is a museum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hunza-state.jpg" alt="Hunza State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ali Atif · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hunza.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1974 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baltit</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Safdar Ali Khan, Muhammad Nazim Khan, Muhammad Jamal Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ismaili Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burushaski</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kanjut, Hunza</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1637&amp;focus=hunza-state" aria-label="Hunza State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6559 867 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hunza State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6879 1123L7567 1068M6879 1123L7364 1602M6879 1123L6905 1198M6879 1123L6778 1519"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0M7364 1602h0M6905 1198h0M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6879 1123h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6879 1123h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1637&amp;focus=hunza-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

